            }
          },
          "title": "<span style=\"font-weight: normal;\">JEEP CAM SENSOR INSTALL 2000-2006</span>",
          "text": "<p>Online video and Youtube particularly is now more popular than cable television.</p>",
          "_tags": [
            "Theme"
          ],
          "buttons": "<a data-app-btn=\"true\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" href=\"https://mobirise.com\">MORE</a>",
          "_name": "msg-box4",
          "_styles": {
            "H3": {
              "color": "#8c8c8c",
              "font-size": "30px",
              "text-align": "center"
            },
            "SPAN": {
              "font-size": "46px",
              "color": "#000000"
            }
          },
          "_anchor": "msg-box4-pu",
          "_sourceTheme": "mobirise3"
        },
        {
          "_params": {
            "paddingTop": "2",
            "paddingBottom": "2",
            "bgColor": "#000000"
          },
          "text": "<p>SETTING UP THE JEEP DISTRIBUTOR</p>",
          "_tags": [
            "Theme"
          ],
          "_name": "content1",
          "_styles": {
            "P": {
              "text-align": "center",
              "font-size": "36px",
              "font-family": "'Oswald', sans-serif",
              "googleLink": "https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Oswald:400,300,700",
              "color": "#efefef"
            }
          },
          "_anchor": "content1-pv",
          "_sourceTheme": "mobirise3"
        },
        {
          "_params": {
            "paddingTop": "2",
            "paddingBottom": "2",
            "bgColor": "#ffffff"
          },
          "text": "<ol><li>Ensure the #1 piston is at TDC on a compression stroke.</li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Look at the pulse ring on the distributor, note that there are two holes.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Insert a 3/16” pin (an allen wrench will do) into the hole at the end of the pulse ring, the&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">alignment pin needs to engage the hole in the distributor. Note: The hole that is “free” is&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">to time the four cylinder engine, do not use for the six cylinder engine.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Pre-position the oil pump to accept the distributor drive.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Insert the distributor, engaging the oil pump. Note: The rotor will not be aligned to the&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">number 1 plug wire on the cap.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Remove alignment pin.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Install and tighten the securing bolt at the base of the distributor.&nbsp;</span></li></ol>",
          "_tags": [
            "Theme"
          ],
          "_name": "content1",
          "_styles": {
            "P": {
              "font-size": "20px",
              "text-align": "left",
              "font-family": "'Oswald', sans-serif",
              "googleLink": "https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Oswald:400,300,700"
            },
            "LI": {
              "text-align": "left",
              "font-size": "20px"
            }
          },
          "_anchor": "content1-pw",
          "_sourceTheme": "mobirise3"
        },
        {
          "_params": {
            "paddingTop": "2",
            "paddingBottom": "2",
            "bgColor": "#000000"
          },
          "text": "<p>JEEP CAMSHAFT BREAK-IN PROCEDURE</p>",
          "_tags": [
            "Theme"
          ],
          "_name": "content1",
          "_styles": {
            "P": {
              "text-align": "center",
              "font-size": "36px",
              "font-family": "'Oswald', sans-serif",
              "googleLink": "https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Oswald:400,300,700",
              "color": "#efefef"
            }
          },
          "_anchor": "content1-px",
          "_sourceTheme": "mobirise3"
        },
        {
          "_params": {
            "paddingTop": "2",
            "paddingBottom": "2",
            "bgColor": "#ffffff"
          },
          "text": "<p>Follow all of the steps in the “<b>Startup Guidelines</b>” before attempting to start your new Jeep engine.</p><p><br></p><p>Get the distributor set properly so the engine will fire off immediately by following the steps in “<b>Setting up the Jeep Distributor</b>”.</p><p><br></p><p>Start the engine and make sure you have oil pressure and a good idle for 15-20 seconds without&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">using any throttle. If it doesn’t idle well, you need to shut the engine down and determine the&nbsp;</span><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">cause of the problem before performing the cam break-in. If this occurs, call the Golen tech line&nbsp;</span><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">@ 800-591-9171, Monday through Friday, 9am to 5pm Eastern time.</span></p><p><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\"><br></span></p><p>Assuming oil pressure and idle are good, bring the engine up to 2000 rpm. You will keep the&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">engine at 2000 rpm for 20 minutes watching for oil or anti-freeze leaks, oil pressure, and ngine&nbsp;</span><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">temperature. Also listen for general engine operation. The lifters may take up to 20 minutes to&nbsp;</span><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">complete pump-up and during this time may make some noise.</span></p><p><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\"><br></span></p><p>If anything needs to be sealed or repaired during break-in, or if the engine overheats, shut the&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">engine down, note how much time is left for the break-in, and repair the issue. Then start the&nbsp;</span><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">engine back up and continue break-in.</span></p><p><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\"><br></span></p><p>After the cam break-in has been completed, you need change both the oil and the oil filter. After&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">the oil and filter have been changed, you may then perform the final inspection steps in “<b>Startup&nbsp;</b></span><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\"><b>Guidelines</b>” and further adjust your timing.&nbsp;</span></p>",
          "_tags": [
            "Theme"
          ],
          "_name": "content1",
          "_styles": {
            "P": {
              "font-size": "20px",
              "text-align": "left",
              "font-family": "'Oswald', sans-serif",
              "googleLink": "https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Oswald:400,300,700"
            }
          },
          "_anchor": "content1-py",
          "_sourceTheme": "mobirise3"
        },
        {
          "_params": {
            "paddingTop": "2",
            "paddingBottom": "2",
            "bgColor": "#000000"
          },
          "text": "<p>HOW TO PRIME THE JEEP OIL PUMP</p>",
          "_tags": [
            "Theme"
          ],
          "_name": "content1",
          "_styles": {
            "P": {
              "text-align": "center",
              "font-size": "36px",
              "font-family": "'Oswald', sans-serif",
              "googleLink": "https://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Oswald:400,300,700",
              "color": "#efefef"
            }
          },
          "_anchor": "content1-pz",
          "_sourceTheme": "mobirise3"
        },
        {
          "_params": {
            "paddingTop": "2",
            "paddingBottom": "2",
            "bgColor": "#ffffff"
          },
          "text": "<p><b>Priming the oil pump in your Jeep engine brings the oil pressure up to normal before the engine is started.</b></p><p><br></p><p>This will protect the engine from premature failure cause by a dry start. Dry start is a condition whereby&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">the engine is initially run for a short time without any lubrication on the bearings. This can wipe and spin&nbsp;</span><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">bearings and cause quick and devastating damage to the engine internals. Priming the engine using the&nbsp;</span>following instructions ensures that the first time it is started there is proper lubrication on all internal parts.</p><p>\n</p><p><br></p><p>Once the engine has been primed and run for the first time, a thin layer of oil always remains on all of the&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">bearings and protects them for those first few critical seconds during startup while the pressure builds up.</span></p><p><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\"><br></span></p><p><b>Priming the oil pump is the most important step to take just before starting your engine for the&nbsp;<span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\">first time.</span></b></p><p><span style=\"line-height: 1.5;\"><br></span></p><ol><li>After you install the engine into the Jeep’s engine compartment, make sure that you install a&nbsp;<span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">new name-brand oil filter and fill the engine with the proper amount of oil. Using the factory&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">oil pan and oil dip-stick, fill to the factory level on the stick.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Install the priming tool into the engine through the distributor hole and attach an electric drill&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">to the priming tool. If you don’t have a priming tool, you may cut the handle off of a flat-head&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">screwdriver and use that to turn the oil pump drive shaft. Be careful, though, as the cut-off&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">end may be sharp.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Next, turn the drill clockwise until you feel the drill load-up. This may take a few minutes, but&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">you will eventually feel the drill start to torque back on your arm. This is a sign that the oil has&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">now been pulled through the oil pump pick-up tube and is now actually pumping through the&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">engine.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">Continue running the drill for another minute after the drill loads-up. This ensures that all of&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">the bearings and moving components have been properly lubricated.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">After priming is completed, install the distributor and start the engine.</span></li><li><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">If you have any questions regarding priming the oil pump, please call the GES tech line at 1-</span><span style=\"font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.5;\">800-591-9171.&nbsp;</span></li></ol>",
          "_tags": [
            "Theme"
          ],
          "_name": "content1",
          "_styles": {
            "P": {
              "font-size": "20px",
              "text-align": "left",
